Date: Thu, 18 Mar 93 18:18:48 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: META: Is an armed society a polite society?

[I just get the digest, so there's a delay in my responding to posts.
I haven't got a digest yet today, but thought I'd send this out before
leaving.]

I'm offering potential polite society members a (semi) line-item veto
on the society rules.  If you are seriously considering joining, but
dislike any of the following rules, tell me, and I'll seriously
consider dumping it.  If this process converges, I'll post the final
rules and those of us still interested (if any) can get started.  

---------------------------------------------------------------------

         AN INVITATION TO JOIN THE POLITE SOCIETY - Draft 3/18/93

  "An armed society is a polite society" - Anon

Tired of flaming and discourteous manners in email and netnews?  Why
not join the "Polite Society"?  Each member is "armed" and may "shoot"
any other member they deem made an especially impolite post.  After a
shot, neither member may post for one week to any newsgroup, list,
BBS, etc.  It is not a perfect indicator, but longstanding members
with few hits have passed at least one substantial test of their
politeness.

FINE POINTS:
- Any person may join by so declaring in a signature to a post.
- Members must use their true names when acting as members, and may
  not join under more than one name.
- One may not shoot until having been a member for two weeks.
- The "target" offending post must have appeared in a context (list, 
  newsgroup, etc.) without an "impolite" warning in the subject line, 
  in a context which has not declared itself potentially impolite.
- The shooter must claim that the target is impolite, not just wrong.
- A "shot" is a freely distributable message, posted in the same
  contexts as the target, which includes a paragraph (20-200 words) 
  explaining the offense, and referring to a "bullet" post.
- A "bullet" post shows that the shooter can do better.  It is made
  by the shooter after the target, in a similar context, dealing with
  a similar social situation, and the shooter claims it is more polite.
- A shooter must forward the target, shot, and bullet to the person hit. 
- Forbidden "posting" includes any email list, newsgroup, BBS, etc.  
- Exceptions are made for one short message explaining one's upcoming
  absence, and for posts required to maintain one's job.
- It takes one month to "reload" for another shot.
- You can't shoot the same person twice, or someone out from a hit.
- Anyone receiving twelve hits is expelled from the Polite Society.
- When asked, a member must acknowledge their membership, tell the year
  they joined and the number of times they have shot others or been hit, 
  and forward copies of all shots and targets they've been involved with.
- All this information, except the copies, should be included in any
  advertizements of membership. For example: 

  "Polite Society Member Since 1993 - 0 Hits, 1 Shot"  or  "PSM93-0/1"

- When one member accuses another of violating these rules, the two
  members must choose another member to judge the case.  
- If no specific judge can be agreed on, they must choose at random
  from members posting in the context (list or newsgroup) closest to 
  where the violation was said to occur.
- For the above offenses, a judge may forbid posting up to one month.  
- For ignoring the dictates of a judge, a new judge may expel someone 
  from membership for up to two years.  
- Since this society has no administration, these rules can't change.
  (You can of course start other societies though.)

This invitation may be freely distributed, and is copyright Robin Hanson 1993.

