From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: BRAIN: Information content

Derek asks about the numbers of synapses...
>
>What I WANTED was to consider all that stuffff.  Help describe it
>in multidimenstional terms.  Plllleeeeeeeeezzzzzzzz?  I need DATA!
>
The number of synapses in the brain is estimated at around 10^14.  Given the
other piece of data I mentioned, that there are 10^11 neurons, that makes about
1000 synapses per neuron.  This is an average number, and of little use,
since synapse/neuron values range from 1 for inhibitory interneurons in the
spinal chord to 10,000 for Purkinje cells of the cerebellum.  (Purkinje cells
are quite numerous, and skew the statistics drastically).

These are just direct connections.  What we really want is the number of
functional connections, i.e. the paths upon which meaningful neural messages
are transmitted.  This is anywhere from 0 to 0.5*10^22 (fully connected) -
quite a range :)  If you were to ask 10 brain experts where they think the
actual number lies, you'd be likely to get about 11 answers...  It's really not
known.

I personally don't value such numerology except to support comparative
statements such as your suggestion that brain structure cannot be completely
genetically determined.  There is a huge literature supporting the notion that
brain development is input-driven, including support from lesion studies,
sensory deprivation etc.

-Paul


