 I don't believe that there is a single plasticity that is safe for a
being to accept. I think that certain brain functions need to have the
stability of an adult mind in order for a person to take responsibilty
for his actions and to keep susceptibilty to dangerous memes from
endangering him.

>  3)   Periodically regress to a plastic state.  This would
>	   probably be my choice, although it also has its problems.

 This might be workable if the person were protected like a child
during the period of plasticity.

>  I'm interested in what choices people would make, or what other
>  solutions they can suggest.  I ask you to refrain from
>  suggestions that we simply "redesign the mind".  For the sake of

>  Assuming also that we want to remain as qualitatively human
>  minds, we must prepare to deal with this handicap.

 The only way to remain qualitatively human is to accept stagnation as in
choice 1. Given the near inevitability of some intelligent life-forms evolving
beyond humanity, this goal is equivalent to choosing inferiority.

 I have another approach which seems to be compatible with your pessimistic
assumptions about our ability to understand the brain. I suggest that uploaded
brains will grow by having new "neurons" added to them, with initial
plasticity comparable to an infant, and that the plasticity would decrease
to that of an adult human brain as the new "modules" prove useful.

