> Anyhow, the point is that Eric's defense of paganism may be incomplete. 

Of course it's incomplete.  There are many other reasons to favor neopaganism.

One of mine is that I've generally found attempts to construct euphopraxies
from the terminology of science not only sterile but perverse --- they tend
to read like shoddy parodies of science rather than exaltations of it.

>From Le Comte's Church of Reason on down, all the attempts have been failures.
The few partial exceptions, like Hubbard's Scientology, got to be exceptions
by aping the structure of initiatory mystery cults, complete with `mystical'
baggage (Hubbard was a Thelemite before founding his cult; many of his
methods and rituals are not-very-well-disguised Crowley).

There are important reasons why neopaganism is succeeding where LeComte
and Hubbard failed.  Neopaganism has better channels into the hindbrain and
unconscious than any pseudo-scientific euphopraxy can generate.

> Rationality may demand that he use not just any memetic vaccine, but the
> one that is most removed from mysticism and most compatible with practical
> and empirical procedures.

I see we're back to using `mysticism' as a term of abuse.  Sigh.

To keep the discussion constructive, let's try to keep our terms straight:

irrationalism = the belief that there are means and modes of understanding
                the objective universe that are more effective than
                experiment interpreted by reason.

mysticism     = the belief that systematic alteration of consciousness
                away from the `normal' waking state can yield critical
                data for understanding and using the *whole* of one's
                mind.  The intentional practice of such alteration.

I am a mystic.  I am not an irrationalist.
-- 
