Dave Lubkin, supporting infinite-duration intellectual property, also
says:

> I don't buy the argument that you are not diminished by someone else
> making a copy of your work, so therefore it should be okay.  Let's 
> suppose I own a weekend cabin with a lake.  I am not diminished by
> your swimming in my lake during the week.  Nonetheless, I may forbid
> you from using my property in that way.
> 
> -- David Lubkin.

Fair enough.  And the converse is true: even *if* you are diminished by
the release of information this is not a justification for intellectual
property.  

On the duration of intellectual 'property' there are two Schelling
points: zero and infinity.  I expect that most PPLs will gravitate
around these two points for intangibles independently.  That is to say
that the duration of tangible property will be infinite (ie no laws
against perpetuities) and intangibles will either be deemed property
(infinite duration) or not (zero duration).  All the intermediate
positions I find too arbitrary to stomach.

