Well, this definition may help you keep *your* terms straight, but it
doesn't jibe with how most English speakers use "mysticism."  WEBSTER'S NEW
COLLEGIATE DICTIONARY defines the term as 

"1: The experience of mystical union or direct communion with ultimate
reality reported by mystics  2: the belief that direct knowledge of God,
spiritual truth, or ultimate reality can be attatined through subjective
experince (as intuition or insight) 3: a: vague speculation: a belief
without sound basis . . . "

You say "I am a mystic.  I am not an irrationalist."  You define the no-no
as:

>irrationalism = the belief that there are means and modes of understanding
>                the objective universe that are more effective than
>                experiment interpreted by reason.

But this sounds a lot like the standard definition of mysticism to me.

I don't mean to impugn (or ratify) any of your theories by this.  I just
want to point out that it behooves us to speak the same language.  Sticking
to standard meanings makes for a nice default rule, I think.  

