> This is an essay I wrote for Computer Science on computers and the developing
> world. Though it is not on future culture, it does contain my thoughts on 
> the politics of networks, and a study of the topic (just btw.). If you
> are not interested, press the delete button. I know its long, I know its not
> strictly futureculture, just don't flame.
> 
> Peter
> 
> TOWARDS A POLITICAL ANALYSIS OF GLOBAL NETWORKING
> 
> Peter van Heusden
> 3rd Year Computer Science essay on HCI
> Topic 5: Computers and the Third World
> 
> ABSTRACT
> 
> This paper considers the question of public access to a global 
> network in developing countries. The research method used, 
> retrieval of source materials from locations on the Internet, 
> stands as a preliminary example of the power of global 
> networks. A case is made for the benefits of global networking, 
> and some problems related to the supply of networks are 
> considered. Within this context, some political questions 
> related to global computer networks (e.g. the nature of current 
> global networks, their use in information distribution and 
> conflict situations, and the nature of an information based 
> society) are considered. Finally, a conclusion is reached over 
> the need for response to the questions of Information 
> Technology on a political level.
> 
> AN ARGUMENT FOR THE AVAILABILITY OF GLOBAL PUBLIC NETWORKS
> 
> In the context of the problems facing developing countries 
> (great shortages of primary resources, e.g. food, energy and 
> housing, as well as a lack of services, such as education and 
> medical care) the existence of such advanced technological 
> structures as computer networks seems a rather questionable 
> extravagance. To justify their existence, once must consider 
> that developing countries, per definition, wish to attain the 
> quality of life which exists in more affluent, developed 
> countries. As the world changes from a primarily industrial 
> power base to a informational one, the need for the developing 
> countries to have the tools of the information age becomes 
> critical. And the computer network is one of the information 
> age's most fundamental tools.
> 
> The network has application in all spectra of public life: In 
> the commercial context, global networks allow for the existence 
> of closer knit, more responsive, corporations which transcend 
> national boundaries, the offices keeping in contact and working 
> together on a global scale with the use of email, as well as 
> more advanced groupware products (like the synchronous writing 
> pads being developed by Xerox PARC). In the general public 
> context, global networks allow one access to remote databases, 
> and systems such as Usenet news allow one to find and work with 
> colleagues in many diverse locations. In the future, 
> distributive databases may be spread across the world's WANs. 
> Finally, in the context of education, global networks allow the 
> possibility of remote education (already being experimented 
> with by Jyrki Kuoppala with his Usenet University [1]) and they 
> provide, as a discursive medium, a link with world debates and 
> trends... a networked country is never an isolated one.
> 
> Of course, all these advantages have their inherent problems, 
> e.g. the existence of large mega-corporations which cross 
> country boundaries allows such corporations an alarming amount 
> of power, and the world trends one has access to on a medium 
> such as Usenet at the moment tend to be biased by the large 
> proportion of American users on the net. However, despite these 
> problems, experience with the current global networks shows 
> that they are of incredible use as tools in all walks of life. 
> The question is not whether to get connected, but how.
> 
> NETWORKS IN DEVELOPMENT: TECHNICAL PROBLEMS
> 
> To state that network access is needed is easy and obvious. To 
> ensure network access is considerably more difficult. 
> Essentially, the problem has 3 major aspects to it: Education, 
> Geography and Responsibility. I shall deal with these in turn.
> 
> EDUCATION
> 
>  FIRST STEPS: PROVING NECESSITY
> 
> If one were to walk into the office of the head of state of a 
> developing country (if one could get that far) and state that 
> his country needs a national network, one will probably be met 
> with considerable scepticism. The government of such a country 
> will typically have access to only outdated computer equipment, 
> put to outdated use (e.g. statistical calculations for census 
> gathering) or none at all. The concept of a connected 
> government using networks in every field, from data gathering 
> to groupwork, is remote in the extreme. Thus, one's first task 
> is to prove the usefulness of a network in the first place. In 
> this context, the concept of critical mass becomes very 
> important.
> 
> Critical mass is the term used to describe the stage a network 
> must reach to be able to grow without constant impetus from its 
> founders. Essentially, a network will start with a small group 
> of developers, often working on the network as a research 
> project (like DARPA did with ARPANET), and it will grow till 
> enough people have been convinced of the usefulness of the 
> project that it is accepted, and expansion of the network is 
> considered a necessary task, rather than a luxury. Such a 
> process can be observed in the development of Uninet-ZA [2].
> 
> Thus, we note that it is necessary to have a dedicated group of 
> individuals to engineer the network towards the state of 
> critical mass. Of course, critical mass is too general a 
> definition for the process of network building. The reality is 
> more often a number of points of critical mass, growing out 
> from the initial one. E.g. whilst it would be fairly easy to 
> convince a Computer Science department to fund and join a 
> network, doing so for the rest of the University is more 
> difficult, and for the rest of the country more difficult 
> still. However, experience in a number of countries shows that 
> this is the method that works [3]. Thus, this educational process 
> must be considered the first goal in the process of founding a 
> national network.
> 
>  TECHNICAL GROUNDING
> 
> If the attainment of critical mass is the first goal of a 
> network, the precondition must be the existence of a dedicated 
> group of technically qualified personnel. With good use of UIs, 
> the front end of the network can be made fairly user friendly, 
> but there is still a need for qualified personnel to deal with 
> the considerable problems of running a network in a developing 
> country. This task is complicated by the use of old software 
> and hardware, bad communication lines, and the simple non-
> existence of some facilities. (E.g. Users of Uninet applauded 
> when the 56kb link was finally installed late last year, but I 
> had the humbling experience of visiting a MUSH where a notice 
> was posted that the administrators apologised for the lag due 
> to their use of a 56kb line. South Africans, justifiably, 
> consider the use of one such line for their whole country a 
> major breakthrough. Similar examples of disparity in equipment 
> abound.) 
> 
> To establish such a group in a developing country is by no 
> means trivial. Apart from the fact that skills are rare, 
> concentrating a number of these experts in one project is a 
> task in itself. However, without such a group, no network can 
> be created or maintained. Luckily, the incentives for such a 
> workgroup are not necessarily financial. Evidence suggests that 
> such technical cores can be formed on the basis of conscience 
> and ideology [4], not merely money. Whatever the incentives, such 
> a group is an absolute necessity.
> 
>  GEOGRAPHICAL ISSUES
> 
> The formation of networks has a definite and very tangible 
> geographical dimension. In this respect, two major problems 
> occur: The low population and technological density of 
> developing countries, and the large distance between some 
> developing countries and the developed world.
> 
> The population density of many developing countries is a 
> problem, in that the aim of a national network must be to 
> supply access to as many potential users as possible. In a 
> country such as South Africa, with only a few, widely separated 
> population centers, and a widely spread out rural population, 
> one is faced with the problem of creating metropolitan networks 
> in urban areas, and then spreading out high cost, low feed 
> networks to rural areas for e.g. agricultural usage, as well as 
> the need to connect all the metropolitan nets with (in 
> information supply terms) useless lengths of connecting cable. 
> To add to the problem, low population density carries with it 
> low density of technology, few telecommunication possibilities 
> (few phone lines, no data lines), and the necessity to 
> investigate alternative methods of connection (E.g. packet 
> radio).
> 
> A similar problem of distribution appears in the context of 
> connection to existing nets in developed countries. By far the 
> largest jump in Uninet is the move from the Rhodes link to the 
> Washington link to the rest of the Internet. Though this is a 
> problem for some developed countries as well (e.g. Australia), 
> by the simple virtue of not being close to the technically 
> developed world, many countries lose out on the spill over 
> effects of technology.
> 
>  INSTITUTIONAL DIMENSIONS
> 
> Finally, one must realise that a network does not come into 
> existence in isolation: a significant question is that of which 
> national institutions are involved in the carrying of data and 
> the supply thereof.
> 
> If one considers the history of networking, one can see that 
> networks have indeed sprouted from many diverse fields. The 
> Internet is the result of DARPA research and the activities of 
> the NSF. Both these organisations exist in a technologically 
> advanced country, and their research was speculation and 
> construction on a grand scale. This can not be hoped for in 
> less wealthy countries. In the developing world, the most 
> common starting points for networks have been the academic 
> community (e.g. Uninet-ZA) and Non Governmental Organisations - 
> NGOs (e.g. the APC networks, of which WorkNet is one example). 
> 
> Of course, the incentive to start a network, and the carrying 
> of data are two different questions. The data carrier is most 
> often a telecommunications monopoly in the developing world, 
> and thus policies may be obscure, bearing no relation to the 
> need for a national network. Telcom's vaguely ununderstandable 
> (and never clearly stated) third-party rules which restrict the 
> growth of South African net are an obvious example.
> 
> To provide impetus to a national network, the founding 
> organisation needs a good financial base. Given the lack of 
> initial interest in a network, it is inconceivable that a 
> dedicated group is founded by the government to form a national 
> network (I have indeed heard of no such cases). Thus, the 
> initial goal of the network is divergent from its final aims. 
> Universities may start networks for academic discussion, and 
> NGOs for the relaying of essential data. If critical mass is 
> reached, however, impetus grows to force these networks beyond 
> their initial scope, as is already happening in South Africa.
> 
> POLITICAL QUESTIONS
> 
> Having sketched some uses for a global network, and mentioned 
> some potential problems, I will now point out the political 
> questions generated by the population of a country having 
> access to a global network. Essentially, this question has two 
> major components: Firstly, the network itself has political 
> use, and a political culture, attached. Secondly, the 
> introduction of networks lead one towards the information age, 
> and the metamorphosis to an information society, with all its 
> corresponding political and social questions.
> 
> NETWORKS AS POLITICAL SPACES
> 
>  LIBERATE INFORMATION
> 
> "...to seek, receive and impart information and ideas through 
> any media and regardless of frontiers." Taken from a possible 
> Charter of Communication Interdependence formulated in a paper 
> by Howard Frederick [5], this is the political essence of the 
> ability of a user of something such as Usenet (Usenet is being 
> used as an example here because of my familiarity with it. 
> Equally well, Fidonet or any other high feed network could 
> serve as an example). If one lets in a network, one is letting 
> in information in quantities too vast to censor, and one aspect 
> of the information available on Usenet is its political 
> freedom. All groups exists: Libertarians, anarchists, 
> communists, revisionists, fundamentalists, and about every 
> shade of every political view commonly held today. Alongside 
> these obviously aligned groups, individual snippets of 
> information abound. From personal experience, I know that it is 
> possible to get access to, amongst others, the formula for C-4 
> plastic explosives, documents on how to make pipe bombs, 
> addresses to send away to to get seeds for hallucinogenic 
> plants and (politically most significant) a transcript of the 
> last words of a group of Turkish leftist revolutionaries who 
> were pinned down and killed by Turkish authorities, via the 
> Internet .
> 
> Thus, if one thing can be said of Usenet, it is that it is 
> free. In allowing participation in such a forum, one is 
> building a questioning, informed populace. There is no Usenet 
> equivalent of the "don't touch that dial!" we so often hear on 
> television, imploring us to remain as slaves to the information 
> feed being transmitted. Usenet is an active participatory 
> medium, and as such, is not overtly controllable by any 
> grouping. Of course, as mentioned before, it might be 
> controlled by forces of simple majorities. At present, Usenet 
> is numerically dominated by Americans, and so picks up an 
> ideological slant from them. This balance is not likely to 
> change towards the developing world in the future, a 
> problematic occurrence. In defense of the net, it can only be 
> said that personal experience has shown that no monolithic 
> ideology dominates all Usenet forums (Soc.feminism can hardly 
> be said to be aligned with a patriarchal regime.).
> 
> Thus, it is clear that a global network is not a politically 
> neutral phenomenon. It is a structure impossible to dominate, 
> difficult to control, and fundamentally empowering on an 
> individual level.
> 
>  SPREAD THE NEWS
> 
> Apart from the generic culture of free information which 
> pervades the net, and which will undeniably reach any country 
> connected to it, the existence of such an essentially 
> uncontrolled information carrier allows for the existence of 
> information created and distributed by groups who are to small 
> to exist in the competitive world of the info business. 
> 
> To illustrate this point, one most note the existence of 
> structures such as the APC (Association for Progressive 
> Communication). Realising that information is carried around 
> the world by a small clique of services (5 carriers deal with 
> 96% of the world's news [6]) who all originate, geographically 
> and ideologically, in the developed world, networks have sprung 
> up across the APC nets, spreading the news from a different 
> perspective. Indeed, the developing world's largest news 
> carrier, Inter Press Services operates on the APC network. 
> Thus, the access to publishing which a global network offers 
> (note that both public domain software packages, as well as a 
> distribution medium, are accessible via networks) is a 
> significant empowering tool in the hands of previously 
> undervalued information producers. (Lorraine Code argues 
> convincingly in 'What can she know?" that the race, class and 
> sex of an information producer is significant. This militates 
> against their equality in the mainstream media. Thus their 
> ability to generate this information freely is significant.)
> 
> Thus, apart from empowering the individual, a network empowers 
> the group, in this case the community of developing countries. 
> Indeed it has been said that in the industrial age, information 
> had power. In the information age, information is power. And a 
> global network is power in the hands of all who have access.
> 
>  THE LAST GATEWAY
> 
> Finally, a network may indeed be the only information source, 
> as the usage of networks during the Gulf war, the Tianemen 
> Square massacre and the recent Soviet coup proved. I shall 
> focus on the events in Russia.
> 
> The main actors in Russia were the members of RELCOM, a network 
> which grew out of customer support for a version of Unix 
> produced by the Demos cooperative. The Demos personnel have 
> been compared to "early entrepreneurs in the personal computer 
> business, before they traded their blue jeans for suits." [7], a 
> description which would suit the members of the "hacker 
> culture" behind such projects as Fido and GNU, as well as a 
> large number of other NGO networks. It is a description typical 
> of the early core of many networks. Demos is a group of 
> professionals, technically well trained, progressive thinkers. 
> (At the time of their founding, free information via networks 
> and private enterprise were unknown, even taboo, in Russia)
> 
> Their nature and existence came to the fore during the coup of 
> August 1991, when one of the first postings, a highly emotive 
> statement of condemnation of the coup, was made by Vadim 
> Antonov, a member of Demos. During the coup, all TV stations 
> and news services were shut down, and RELCOM, along with 
> GlasNet, became the route for information to enter the Soviet 
> union, and the connection between activities in the Soviet 
> cities. On August the 21st, 13,159 messages passed along the 
> RELCOM link to Finland, more than twice the normal volume.
> 
> This action as a tool of direct resistance is an obvious 
> application of a global network. Global network creates global 
> community, and in a global community, everyone's dirty washing 
> gets a good airing. The foundation of totalitarian societies is 
> in the restriction of information, a situation which cannot 
> continue with a national and global network in place. Of 
> course, the existence of such a network is by no means a 
> guarantee of success. One message of encouragement sent to 
> Antonov and his colleagues during the coup came from Jie Liang, 
> who had participated in a similar forum during the events at 
> Tianemen Square. Though the students of Tianemen failed, a 
> network feed certainly helped to get information to the outside 
> world when all other channels were closed.
> 
> INFORMATION SOCIETY AND THE GLOBAL COMMUNITY
> 
> As I have noted, a global network creates a global society. 
> When discussing your favourite compiler the comp. hierarchy, or 
> ideology in a soc. group, your country of origin is most often 
> a secondary matter, to a great degree transparent. Thus, the 
> community brought into existence by a global network such as 
> Usenet transcends national boundaries, undermining the concept 
> of nation, or nationalism per se. In fact, this is one reason 
> why developing countries need to have access to networks: The 
> global network community is a real one, and exclusion from this 
> community is a very real problem.
> 
> A problem created by this information community, however, is 
> intolerance of the new class boundaries of the information age. 
> Just as the control of physical means of production formed the 
> underpinnings of the class structure of the industrial age, so 
> the class structure in the information age will be based on the 
> control of information and information technology. This 
> generates a scope for the creation of new elites on two axes: 
> the elite of information owners, who control the structures of 
> the information economy by pure ownership rights, and the elite 
> of the technologically knowledgeable.
> 
> The ownership of information is a problematic issue in itself. 
> The creators of GNU, for instance, dispute the need for any 
> ownership of information at all. Seen in terms of the community 
> of developing countries, the ownership of information by the 
> developed countries limits the possibilities of further 
> development of their own information economies. As I have said 
> before, in the information age information is power, and the 
> ownership of information is thus inherently a political issue. 
> Whether the info-rich countries act with their knowledge as 
> they have with their material resources, hoarding it to keep 
> the capitalist structures working, has yet to be seen, but the 
> succession of look and feel lawsuits, patents on basic 
> information structures, and increased paranoia about software 
> piracy bode ill for the developing world.
> 
> The people who can possibly break this deadlock are the base 
> creators of information. As opposed to the situation in the 
> industrial world, the information society relies upon highly 
> trained people as its prime producers, and these people form a 
> volatile elite of their own. They control the knowledge that 
> underpins the information society, but also contain some seeds 
> of the forces militating to free information. It is in the 
> interest of the power structures of the info-rich to keep these 
> personnel on their side, dutifully producing information for 
> their profit, but, possibly due to their intellectual 
> background, these prime producers have spawned a number of 
> movements of refusal, such as differing concepts of how gain 
> should be made from the information economy (as embodied by the 
> Free Software Foundation's principles with GNU) and a sub-
> culture which espouses the freeing of information, the "hacker 
> culture". The frenzied raids on suspected crackers, using 
> excessive force, which have occurred in recent times in the USA 
> show that power structures are obviously threatened by this 
> sub-culture.
> 
> The existence of these elites of technology and information is 
> a necessary result of the structure of capitalist society. The 
> use of networks in a small way can break down some of the 
> barriers, by providing channels for the free flow of 
> information and free software, but the balance of this question 
> of power in information resides not within any struggle of the 
> developing world, but rather in the result of the struggle 
> between forces in the developed world. The structure of the 
> information age is something the developing world will have to 
> react to, whether it wants to or not.
> 
> CONCLUSION
> 
> Considering the situation today, we may note a couple of 
> points.
> 
> 1.	The developing world needs networks to gain and maintain a 
> 	place in the company of nations. This will be a prerequisite 
> 	for its survival. Already most of the developing world lags 
> 	behind, as the remains of the agricultural age in an 
> 	industrial world. To lag behind even further will result in 
> 	a fundamental lack of understanding and thus a lack of 
> 	assistance on the part of the developed world.
> 2.	The installation of networks is a process fraught with 
> 	problems in the educational, geographic and institutional 
> 	domains.
> 3.	Access to networks is a political question. On the part of 
> 	the developing world, it must be noted that the culture of 
> 	the current global computer networks, which will most 
> 	probably continue to pervade network society, does not 
> 	suffer tyrants lightly. On the part of the developed world, 
> 	it must be noted that the existence of an information 
> 	society will lead to elites, and struggles within the 
> 	developed world over the control  and structure of the power 
> 	of information. In this context, countries without  network 
> 	access will truly be in the Third World, another world, 
> 	separate from the First World by a great social gap.
> 
> Information is power. When we perceive this fact, when we 
> perceive the politics of networks, we are left with only one 
> question. As political animals, how do we respond?
